l":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/TinyPhi/OpenWind/commit/dafaa4630047b6683c9606be3eaa72a05e2a8773","node_id":"C_kwDOScMdh9oAKGRhZmFhNDYzMDA0N2I2NjgzYzk2MDZiZTNlYWE3MmEwNWUyYTg3NzM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/TinyPhi/OpenWind/commit/9daed72952a86b9eed8ecf12277b7a69896b5973","sha":"9daed72952a86b9eed8ecf12277b7a69896b5973","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/TinyPhi/OpenWind/commits/9daed72952a86b9eed8ecf12277b7a69896b5973"}],"sha":"dafaa4630047b6683c9606be3eaa72a05e2a8773","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/TinyPhi/OpenWind/commits/dafaa4630047b6683c9606be3eaa72a05e2a8773"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/118787765?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/imRahul05/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/imRahul05/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/imRahul05/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/imRahul05/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/imRahul05","id":118787765,"login":"imRahul05","node_id":"U_kgDOBxSOtQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/imRahul05/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/imRahul05/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/imRahul05/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/imRahul05/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/imRahul05/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/imRahul05","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/TinyPhi/OpenWind/commits/9daed72952a86b9eed8ecf12277b7a69896b5973/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-07T07:37:05Z","email":"118787765+imRahul05@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Rahul Kumar"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-07T07:37:05Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"perf(admin-ui): share /users and notification requests and load a workflow''s first tickets with its shell (2/5, split from #772) (#783)\n\n* feat(admin-ui): add a session-end signal so per-identity caches reset on logout\n\nThe follow-up commits add module-level caches (the /users list, in-flight\nnotification requests). Those must never survive the identity that filled\nthem: a user logging out and a different user logging in, in the same tab,\nmust not be served the first user''s data.\n\nAdd src/lib/session-events.ts, a dependency-free onSessionEnd /\nemitSessionEnd pub/sub. It deliberately does not import authProvider, which\nwould create an api <-> authProvider import cycle. Each listener runs in\nits own try/catch so a failing cache reset can never block logout or the\nother listeners.\n\nauthProvider emits it when the session really ends:\n- logout() emits first, before the async sign-out steps, so no request that\n  finishes mid-logout can be answered from the previous identity''s cache.\n  If the sign-out then fails, the only cost is a refetch.\n- userManager''s addUserUnloaded and addAccessTokenExpired events.\n- silentRefresh() when signinSilent resolves without an access token, and\n  when it rejects with oidc-client-ts'' ErrorResponse (invalid_grant,\n  login_required, ...), i.e. the authorization server rejected the refresh.\n  A network error, timeout or other plain Error is transient: the session\n  may still be valid, so it returns null without emitting and no caches are\n  flushed. (In 3.5, JsonService throws ErrorResponse only for an OAuth error\n  body; fetch failures are TypeError and timeouts ErrorTimeout.)\n\nTests cover listener isolation and unsubscribe, every emit site, and that a\nnetwork Error, TypeError and ErrorTimeout do not emit.\n\nPart 2 of 5 of the split of #772.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(admin-ui): share one cached /users request and surface its failure\n\nSeveral components mounted on one page each fetched /api/users on their\nown, and a failed request was swallowed into an empty list that looked\nlike \"no users\".\n\nAdd fetchUsersShared() in lib/use-users.ts:\n- Single-flight: callers that arrive while a request is in flight share its\n  promise, so one HTTP request leaves the browser.\n- A 60 s in-memory cache (USERS_CACHE_TTL_MS), short enough that a newly\n  created or renamed user shows up in pickers without a hard reload.\n- A generation counter bumped by clearUsersCache(), so a request that\n  started before the clear (for example before a logout) can never\n  repopulate the cache afterwards. clearUsersCache is registered with\n  onSessionEnd, so the next login in the same tab never sees the previous\n  identity''s user list.\n- It rejects when the request fails. Failures are not cached and the\n  in-flight slot is cleared, so the next caller retries. Callers that only\n  need the list for display names can fall back to [] themselves.\n\nThe cache is not keyed by tenant: it assumes one identity per tab, and\nswitching identity requires a full logout, which clears it. If in-session\ntenant switching is ever added, key it by tenant.\n\nuseUsers() now returns { users, loading, error }, initialises from a fresh\ncache entry without a request, and ignores results after unmount.\nUserRefPicker, the only component that would otherwise show an empty\ndropdown on failure, renders \"Couldn''t load users\" with a Retry button.\n\nTests cover single-flight, TTL boundaries, the generation guard across\nclear/logout, failure not being cached, concurrent callers all seeing a\nfailure, useUsers error then recovery, and the picker''s error and Retry.\n\nPart 2 of 5 of the split of #772.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(admin-ui): share in-flight notification list and unread-count requests\n\nThe notification bell and other consumers each issued their own\nGET /notifications (first page) and /notifications/unread-count when they\nmounted or refreshed at the same time.\n\nlistNotifications() without a cursor and getUnreadCount() now return the\npromise of an identical request that is already in flight, and clear their\nslot when it settles (guarded by an identity check so a newer request\nisn''t cleared by an older one finishing). Paged requests with a cursor are\nnever shared. Only requests in flight at the same moment are shared; there\nis no result cache, so nothing can go stale.\n\nTwo cases must not join an older request:\n- After markNotificationRead() or markAllNotificationsRead(), a read that\n  started before the mutation may carry pre-mutation data, so the slots are\n  cleared and the next caller issues a fresh request.\n- On session end (onSessionEnd), so a request from the previous identity\n  can''t be handed to the next one.\n\nTests cover sharing for the first page and for the unread count, and that a\nread issued after markNotificationRead() or markAllNotificationsRead() does\nnot join a request that started before the mutation (list and count, both\nmutations).\n\nPart 2 of 5 of the split of #772.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(admin-ui): fetch a workflow''s first tickets with its shell, and retry on failure\n\nWorkflowRecords loaded in four sequential round-trips: the workflow slug\nlookup, the workflow detail, the fields and users, and only then the\ntickets, because the ticket-list effect did not run until the shell had set\nworkflowId and entityTypeId.\n\nThe shell effect now fetches the fields, the shared /users list and the\nfirst page of tickets together in one Promise.all after the workflow\nresolves, taking the ticket load from the fourth round-trip to the third.\nThe request is built by an extracted, exported buildRecordsRequest() (it\nalso resolves the caller''s role and workflow-admin status the same way the\nlist effect always did), and both effects apply responses through a shared\nparseRecordsResponse(). A /users failure falls back to an empty list, since\nthe list is only used for display names and filtering; it must not fail the\nwhole page.\n\nTo avoid an immediate duplicate request, appliedRecordsUrlRef records the\nURL whose response is currently shown in `records`, and the list effect\nskips exactly that URL. The ref is written only after a response is applied\nand cleared on failure, which fixes three problems a naive \"already loaded\"\nflag has:\n- A failed first-page request used to leave an empty board that looked like\n  \"no tickets\" and was never retried. The shell now leaves the ref unset on\n  failure, so the list effect retries the URL, and if that also fails the\n  existing error state is shown.\n- A dependency change that kept the URL (currentUserId resolving, a new Set\n  identity) cancelled the in-flight request and then skipped the refetch,\n  losing the response. Because the ref is only set on success, the next run\n  fetches.\n- Toggling a filter away and back before its request resolved left the\n  \"refreshing\" indicator on forever, because the cancelled run''s finally\n  never cleared it. The skip path now clears it.\n\nThe shell effect also ignores responses after the slug changes, and\nOrgUser.email is nullable to match TenantUser.\n\nTests cover the retry and the double-failure error, exactly one records\nrequest on a normal load, the away-and-back filter toggle, the same-URL\ndependency change, /users failing, and buildRecordsRequest routing and\nfilters.\n\nPart 2 of 5 of the split of #772.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(admin-ui): share the users request in ticket detail and skip loads after a failed record\n\nCustomerRecordDetail fetched /api/users itself on every load, even when\nother components on the page (the user-ref pickers) request the same list,\nand after loadRecord() it fetched comments, attachments and tags whether\nor not the record loaded.\n\nRead the users from the shared single-flight cache (fetchUsersShared),\nkeeping the existing fallback to an empty list when it fails.\n\nloadRecord() now resolves to whether the record loaded. The effect keyed on\nthe record id requests comments, attachments and tags together only when it\ndid, and drops the follow-up if the id changes before the record resolves.\nA 403 or 404 record therefore no longer fires three more failing requests\nfor a ticket the viewer can''t see, and a stale response for the previous\nrecord can''t start loads for the next one.\n\nTests cover the core view still rendering when comments and attachments\nfail with 500, and no comment, attachment or tag requests after a 404 or\n403 record.\n\nPart 2 of 5 of the split of #772.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(admin-ui): add changelog and week-log entries for request deduplication\n\nRecord the shared /users cache and its failure surfacing, the in-flight\nnotification dedup, the faster and retrying workflow records load, and the\nsession-end signal in CHANGELOG.md, and add a dated week-log entry under\ndocs/tracker/week-log/ per the repo''s delivery flow. The week-log notes the\nsingle-identity-per-tab assumption of the users cache.\n\nPart 2 of 5 of the split of #772.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"52b5dc1c55e767f4aaf581a856ae699cb0b7ebd5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/TinyPhi/OpenWind/git/trees/52b5dc1c55e767f4aaf581a856ae699cb0b7ebd5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/TinyPhi/OpenWind/git/commits/9daed72952a86b9eed8ecf12277b7a69896b5973","verification":{"payload":"tree 52b5dc1c55e767f4aaf581a856ae699cb0b7ebd5\nparent b1c9496501aa9d201e4600602b4875785aecedaa\nauthor Rahul Kumar <118787765+imRahul05@users.noreply.github.com> 1791358625 +0530\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791358625 +0530\n\nperf(admin-ui): share /users and notification requests and load a workflow''s first tickets with its shell (2/5, split from #772) (#783)\n\n* feat(admin-ui): add a session-end signal so per-identity caches reset on logout\n\nThe follow-up commits add module-level caches (the /users list, in-flight\nnotification requests). Those must never survive the identity that filled\nthem: a user logging out and a different user logging in, in the same tab,\nmust not be served the first user''s data.\n\nAdd src/lib/session-events.ts, a dependency-free onSessionEnd /\nemitSessionEnd pub/sub. It deliberately does not import authProvider, which\nwould create an api <-> authProvider import cycle. Each listener runs in\nits own try/catch so a failing cache reset can never block logout or the\nother listeners.\n\nauthProvider emits it when the session really ends:\n- logout() emits first, before the async sign-out steps, so no request that\n  finishes mid-logout can be answered from the previous identity''s cache.\n  If the sign-out then fails, the only cost is a refetch.\n- userManager''s addUserUnloaded and addAccessTokenExpired events.\n- silentRefresh() when signinSilent resolves without an access token, and\n  when it rejects with oidc-client-ts'' ErrorResponse (invalid_grant,\n  login_required, ...), i.e. the authorization server rejected the refresh.\n  A network error, timeout or other plain Error is transient: the session\n  may still be valid, so it returns null without emitting and no caches are\n  flushed. (In 3.5, JsonService throws ErrorResponse only for an OAuth error\n  body; fetch failures are TypeError and timeouts ErrorTimeout.)\n\nTests cover listener isolation and unsubscribe, every emit site, and that a\nnetwork Error, TypeError and ErrorTimeout do not emit.\n\nPart 2 of 5 of the split of #772.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(admin-ui): share one cached /users request and surface its failure\n\nSeveral components mounted on one page each fetched /api/users on their\nown, and a failed request was swallowed into an empty list that looked\nlike \"no users\".\n\nAdd fetchUsersShared() in lib/use-users.ts:\n- Single-flight: callers that arrive while a request is in flight share its\n  promise, so one HTTP request leaves the browser.\n- A 60 s in-memory cache (USERS_CACHE_TTL_MS), short enough that a newly\n  created or renamed user shows up in pickers without a hard reload.\n- A generation counter bumped by clearUsersCache(), so a request that\n  started before the clear (for example before a logout) can never\n  repopulate the cache afterwards. clearUsersCache is registered with\n  onSessionEnd, so the next login in the same tab never sees the previous\n  identity''s user list.\n- It rejects when the request fails. Failures are not cached and the\n  in-flight slot is cleared, so the next caller retries. Callers that only\n  need the list for display names can fall back to [] themselves.\n\nThe cache is not keyed by tenant: it assumes one identity per tab, and\nswitching identity requires a full logout, which clears it. If in-session\ntenant switching is ever added, key it by tenant.\n\nuseUsers() now returns { users, loading, error }, initialises from a fresh\ncache entry without a request, and ignores results after unmount.\nUserRefPicker, the only component that would otherwise show an empty\ndropdown on failure, renders \"Couldn''t load users\" with a Retry button.\n\nTests cover single-flight, TTL boundaries, the generation guard across\nclear/logout, failure not being cached, concurrent callers all seeing a\nfailure, useUsers error then recovery, and the picker''s error and Retry.\n\nPart 2 of 5 of the split of #772.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(admin-ui): share in-flight notification list and unread-count requests\n\nThe notification bell and other consumers each issued their own\nGET /notifications (first page) and /notifications/unread-count when they\nmounted or refreshed at the same time.\n\nlistNotifications() without a cursor and getUnreadCount() now return the\npromise of an identical request that is already in flight, and clear their\nslot when it settles (guarded by an identity check so a newer request\nisn''t cleared by an older one finishing). Paged requests with a cursor are\nnever shared. Only requests in flight at the same moment are shared; there\nis no result cache, so nothing can go stale.\n\nTwo cases must not join an older request:\n- After markNotificationRead() or markAllNotificationsRead(), a read that\n  started before the mutation may carry pre-mutation data, so the slots are\n  cleared and the next caller issues a fresh request.\n- On session end (onSessionEnd), so a request from the previous identity\n  can''t be handed to the next one.\n\nTests cover sharing for the first page and for the unread count, and that a\nread issued after markNotificationRead() or markAllNotificationsRead() does\nnot join a request that started before the mutation (list and count, both\nmutations).\n\nPart 2 of 5 of the split of #772.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(admin-ui): fetch a workflow''s first tickets with its shell, and retry on failure\n\nWorkflowRecords loaded in four sequential round-trips: the workflow slug\nlookup, the workflow detail, the fields and users, and only then the\ntickets, because the ticket-list effect did not run until the shell had set\nworkflowId and entityTypeId.\n\nThe shell effect now fetches the fields, the shared /users list and the\nfirst page of tickets together in one Promise.all after the workflow\nresolves, taking the ticket load from the fourth round-trip to the third.\nThe request is built by an extracted, exported buildRecordsRequest() (it\nalso resolves the caller''s role and workflow-admin status the same way the\nlist effect always did), and both effects apply responses through a shared\nparseRecordsResponse(). A /users failure falls back to an empty list, since\nthe list is only used for display names and filtering; it must not fail the\nwhole page.\n\nTo avoid an immediate duplicate request, appliedRecordsUrlRef records the\nURL whose response is currently shown in `records`, and the list effect\nskips exactly that URL. The ref is written only after a response is applied\nand cleared on failure, which fixes three problems a naive \"already loaded\"\nflag has:\n- A failed first-page request used to leave an empty board that looked like\n  \"no tickets\" and was never retried. The shell now leaves the ref unset on\n  failure, so the list effect retries the URL, and if that also fails the\n  existing error state is shown.\n- A dependency change that kept the URL (currentUserId resolving, a new Set\n  identity) cancelled the in-flight request and then skipped the refetch,\n  losing the response. Because the ref is only set on success, the next run\n  fetches.\n- Toggling a filter away and back before its request resolved left the\n  \"refreshing\" indicator on forever, because the cancelled run''s finally\n  never cleared it. The skip path now clears it.\n\nThe shell effect also ignores responses after the slug changes, and\nOrgUser.email is nullable to match TenantUser.\n\nTests cover the retry and the double-failure error, exactly one records\nrequest on a normal load, the away-and-back filter toggle, the same-URL\ndependency change, /users failing, and buildRecordsRequest routing and\nfilters.\n\nPart 2 of 5 of the split of #772.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(admin-ui): share the users request in ticket detail and skip loads after a failed record\n\nCustomerRecordDetail fetched /api/users itself on every load, even when\nother components on the page (the user-ref pickers) request the same list,\nand after loadRecord() it fetched comments, attachments and tags whether\nor not the record loaded.\n\nRead the users from the shared single-flight cache (fetchUsersShared),\nkeeping the existing fallback to an empty list when it fails.\n\nloadRecord() now resolves to whether the record loaded. The effect keyed on\nthe record id requests comments, attachments and tags together only when it\ndid, and drops the follow-up if the id changes before the record resolves.\nA 403 or 404 record therefore no longer fires three more failing requests\nfor a ticket the viewer can''t see, and a stale response for the previous\nrecord can''t start loads for the next one.\n\nTests cover the core view still rendering when comments and attachments\nfail with 500, and no comment, attachment or tag requests after a 404 or\n403 record.\n\nPart 2 of 5 of the split of #772.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(admin-ui): add changelog and week-log entries for request deduplication\n\nRecord the shared /users cache and its failure surfacing, the in-flight\nnotification dedup, the faster and retrying workflow records load, and the\nsession-end signal in CHANGELOG.md, and add a dated week-log entry under\ndocs/tracker/week-log/ per the repo''s delivery flow. The week-log notes the\nsingle-identity-per-tab assumption of the users cache.\n\nPart 2 of 5 of the split of #772.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP